#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111877 :tutorial/content "We can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the **product rule** for logarithms.\n\nThe **product rule** states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the product rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡(3) & ={log}_{5}⁡(4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." :tutorial/id #uuid "a915130a-f6b9-5fec-a625-3d6d987255ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2782 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
